--TEST--
`->` on a receiver whose declared type can never hold an object is `E0495` where it is written, and a union with a member that is not a class takes the same code — `rule:types/a-declared-type-answers-before-the-program-runs`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// PHP warns and yields `null` for every receiver below. A declared type
// makes the answer knowable before the program runs, so this is row 8's
// rule ("nothing makes an absent thing read as a zero value") at the one
// storage kind a *type* already answers — `rule:types/a-declared-type-answers-before-the-program-runs`.
final class Box { public string $name = "boxed"; public function constructor() { } }
final class Circle { public string $name = "round"; public function constructor() { } }

int $count = 5;
echo $count->name, "\n";

string $text = "s";
echo $text->length, "\n";

array<string> $rows = ["k" => "v"];
echo $rows->k, "\n";

// A union with a member that is not a class has no one property set to
// resolve against, so it is the same refusal rather than a second one.
Box|int $either = new Box();
echo $either->name, "\n";

// `mixed` is deliberately *not* here: it is `rule:types/conversion`'s one unchecked
// position and defers to a run-time throw, which the companion case pins.
mixed $deferred = new Box();
echo $deferred->name, "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0495]: a property cannot be reached through `int`
   --> %s:10:6
   |
10 | echo $count->name, "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^ this receiver's type declares no properties
   = help: only an object has properties. Test the value with `$x is Box` first. Or declare it `mixed`, and a missing property throws an error when the program runs

error[E0495]: a property cannot be reached through `string`
   --> %s:13:6
   |
13 | echo $text->length, "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this receiver's type declares no properties
   = help: only an object has properties. Test the value with `$x is Box` first. Or declare it `mixed`, and a missing property throws an error when the program runs

error[E0495]: a property cannot be reached through `array<string>`
   --> %s:16:6
   |
16 | echo $rows->k, "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^ this receiver's type declares no properties
   = help: only an object has properties. Test the value with `$x is Box` first. Or declare it `mixed`, and a missing property throws an error when the program runs

error[E0495]: a property cannot be reached through `int|Box`
   --> %s:21:6
   |
21 | echo $either->name, "\n";
   |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this receiver's type declares no properties
   = help: only an object has properties. Test the value with `$x is Box` first. Or declare it `mixed`, and a missing property throws an error when the program runs

